<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- The Play build's launch theme on Android 12 and later: Wear OS 4 and up, which is every
         Wear OS version that has a platform splash screen.

         The launcher icon is a choice here (Settings -> Appearance -> Launcher icon): the watch
         enables one of the <activity-alias> entries, and an alias cannot carry a theme of its
         own - it takes the one of the activity it points at. A theme that names a splash icon
         therefore names the same icon for every alias, which is how the watch went on opening
         with the gradient icon after Cream had been chosen.

         So this theme names none. With no icon of its own the system draws the launcher icon of
         the component that was opened - the alias, whose android:icon is the chosen style's -
         round, at the size Wear OS gives every app's launch (48 dp, which some versions then grow
         to 60) on the black window. That is what WO-V15 asks for (a splash icon that matches the
         launcher icon), and it follows the choice with nothing to keep in step.

         Leaving the item out is not enough, and the difference is easy to miss: the androidx
         Theme.SplashScreen this inherits from sets the platform attribute to its own default,
         the generic Android icon. An explicit @null is what hands the choice back to the system.
         The library's own attribute (windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon, no android: prefix) is
         deliberately left as it is.

         Below Android 12 (Wear OS 3) there is no platform splash screen. The library draws the
         icon into the window background from the theme in values/, which can only be one fixed
         icon: the default style's, see splash_screen.xml. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.Starting" parent="Base.AppTheme.Starting">
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@null</item>
    </style>
</resources>
